But once that's
done, we don't need

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:20.120
to wait for an email to
go out to actually send

00:10:20.120 --> 00:10:21.690
a confirmation to the user.

00:10:21.690 --> 00:10:26.180
So we send a message out on
Pub/Sub to send an email.

00:10:26.180 --> 00:10:29.660
And we have this generic email
server that creates a client

00:10:29.660 --> 00:10:32.030
and sends a request
with the data,

00:10:32.030 --> 00:10:34.220
to, that's who the
email is going to,

00:10:34.220 --> 00:10:37.550
from, that's who is e-mailing
it, the subject, and the body.

00:10:37.550 --> 00:10:40.380
And this can be actually reused
throughout our application.

00:10:40.380 --> 00:10:41.900
So the exact same
Cloud Function is

00:10:41.900 --> 00:10:44.210
running to send an email
when you subscribe.

00:10:44.210 --> 00:10:47.030
And it's also sending an
email every single day,

00:10:47.030 --> 00:10:48.590
whenever new facts go out.

00:10:48.590 --> 00:10:51.140
And by splitting it out
into a Cloud Function,

00:10:51.140 --> 00:10:52.700
it's able to scale
up with our load

00:10:52.700 --> 00:10:54.650
as we have more
people subscribing.

00:10:54.650 --> 00:10:57.154
It's able to be used
for different things.

00:10:57.154 --> 00:10:58.820
And it's able to be
its own code that we

00:10:58.820 --> 00:11:00.470
can think about independently.

00:11:00.470 --> 00:11:03.184
And if we ever want to change
our email service, for example,

00:11:03.184 --> 00:11:04.850
we could just change
the Cloud Function.

00:11:04.850 --> 00:11:07.954
As long as we adhere to the same
API, the rest of the services

00:11:07.954 --> 00:11:09.120
don't need to change at all.

00:11:11.325 --> 00:11:13.700
AJA HAMMERLY: And so we talked
about adding new features.

00:11:13.700 --> 00:11:15.310
And one of the things
I wanted to add

00:11:15.310 --> 00:11:18.060
was a Cat Facts bot, because
I really like chat bots.

00:11:18.060 --> 00:11:19.480
They're a lot of fun.

00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:23.240
And our team uses Hangouts
Chat, which is super cool.

00:11:23.240 --> 00:11:24.970
And so I've written
a Hangouts Chat bot.

00:11:24.970 --> 00:11:26.344
And one of the
interesting things

00:11:26.344 --> 00:11:28.540
is that both the
mailer and the bot

00:11:28.540 --> 00:11:30.550
use the same underlying
Cat Facts service.

00:11:30.550 --> 00:11:34.770
And all the Hangouts Chat bot
does is it reformats the fact

00:11:34.770 --> 00:11:37.180
and picture a
payload into the form

00:11:37.180 --> 00:11:39.190
that Hangouts Chat requires.

00:11:39.190 --> 00:11:42.070
All I have to do is call out
to the Cat Facts service,

00:11:42.070 --> 00:11:45.580
get a fact back, and
then send back a 200

00:11:45.580 --> 00:11:47.960
with the JSON package the
exact way that the Hangouts

00:11:47.960 --> 00:11:49.510
Chat expects it.

00:11:49.510 --> 00:11:51.040
And I had a chat bot.

00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:53.260
It was really fast
and really easy.

00:11:53.260 --> 00:11:56.410
And because it's running in
Cloud Functions, if 100,000

00:11:56.410 --> 00:11:59.440
people hit my Hangouts Chat
bot to get their Cat Facts

00:11:59.440 --> 00:12:01.090
at the same time, it's fine.

00:12:01.090 --> 00:12:03.670
And if no one's using
my Cat Facts chat

00:12:03.670 --> 00:12:06.280
bot, because no one wants
cat facts right now,

00:12:06.280 --> 00:12:07.030
that's also fine.

00:12:07.030 --> 00:12:08.696
Cloud Functions will
scale automatically

00:12:08.696 --> 00:12:10.342
to what I need at the time.

00:12:10.342 --> 00:12:11.800
And so I really
liked it, because I

00:12:11.800 --> 00:12:14.410
don't know if Cat Facts
is going to take off.

00:12:14.410 --> 00:12:18.560
Maybe people don't want cat
facts in their chat bots.

00:12:18.560 --> 00:12:20.380
But Myles is going
to actually show you

00:12:20.380 --> 00:12:22.360
that chat bot right now.

00:12:22.360 --> 00:12:25.150
And you guys are going to
see pictures of my cats.

00:12:25.150 --> 00:12:28.420
MYLES BORINS: So here you can
see, we are in Hangouts Chat.

00:12:28.420 --> 00:12:30.550
And we've got a chat
going with the bot.

00:12:30.550 --> 00:12:34.920
And I could go like,
hey, bot, give me a cat.

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:37.170
And the bot's is going to
think about it for a second.

00:12:37.170 --> 00:12:39.214
It's making a call to
the Cloud Function.

00:12:39.214 --> 00:12:41.130
It's getting the data
from the Cloud Function.

00:12:41.130 --> 00:12:43.410
It comes back with
a cat and a fact,

00:12:43.410 --> 00:12:47.220
that each cat and owner have
their own unique vocabulary.

00:12:47.220 --> 00:12:52.770
And we can message it again,
hey, more cats [? plx. ?]

00:12:52.770 --> 00:12:56.190
And we give it a second to think
about a new cat and a new fact.

00:12:56.190 --> 00:12:58.262
And it gets back to us--

00:12:58.262 --> 00:13:01.970
argh-- with the same
cat and the same fact.

00:13:01.970 --> 00:13:04.190
And that's how you know
that this is a real demo.

00:13:06.860 --> 00:13:07.720
Back to you, Aja.

00:13:10.528 --> 00:13:14.620
AJA HAMMERLY: So what happened
there is I had a time out.

00:13:14.620 --> 00:13:17.440
I did not actually officially
put enough time in my chat bot

00:13:17.440 --> 00:13:20.200
to do the update on my query,
because we're trying to pull

00:13:20.200 --> 00:13:21.870
the least recently used.

00:13:21.870 --> 00:13:23.890
I spent some time
debugging that yesterday.

00:13:23.890 --> 00:13:26.590
But I'm sorry that you guys
saw the broken version.

00:13:26.590 --> 00:13:27.840
But you know it's real.

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:30.256
So Myles is going talk a little
bit about the architecture

00:13:30.256 --> 00:13:31.240
that we ended up with.

00:13:31.240 --> 00:13:32.865
And then, we're going
to talk about one

00:13:32.865 --> 00:13:36.002
of the last compute options
you have available on GCP.

00:13:36.002 --> 00:13:37.960
MYLES BORINS: So we've
been talking through all

00:13:37.960 --> 00:13:38.860
these different services.

00:13:38.860 --> 00:13:41.526
But we thought that this diagram
would kind of give a clear idea

00:13:41.526 --> 00:13:42.599
of how we architected it.

00:13:42.599 --> 00:13:44.140
So we have a front-end
that's written

00:13:44.140 --> 00:13:47.020
in Node.js and Express, that's
running on App Engine, that's

00:13:47.020 --> 00:13:48.370
taking user requests.

00:13:48.370 --> 00:13:52.000
And when you sign up, it's
adding a user to big table.

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:54.310
And then, it's
requesting a cat fact

00:13:54.310 --> 00:13:55.900
from the Cat Fact function.

00:13:55.900 --> 00:14:00.760
And then, it's submitting
an event to Pub/Sub

00:14:00.760 --> 00:14:02.440
to let it know to send an email.

00:14:02.440 --> 00:14:05.410
Pub/Sub is then
triggering the function

00:14:05.410 --> 00:14:08.290
that's listening for new email
events and emailing it out.

00:14:08.290 --> 00:14:13.490
Now, the Task Scheduler
is another App Engine app.

00:14:13.490 --> 00:14:16.120
If you didn't know, you
can actually make Cron jobs

00:14:16.120 --> 00:14:22.780
using App Engine to set it up to
execute App Engine REST calls,

00:14:22.780 --> 00:14:24.370
based on certain
amounts of time.

00:14:24.370 --> 00:14:26.674
So the Task Scheduler's set
to run on a daily basis.

00:14:26.674 --> 00:14:28.840
Every single day, it gets
the list of all the people

00:14:28.840 --> 00:14:30.070
who have subscribed.

00:14:30.070 --> 00:14:33.880
And then, it sets a new
task in the Pub/Sub queue

00:14:33.880 --> 00:14:37.060
for every single person
to send them a new fact.

00:14:37.060 --> 00:14:41.119
The emailer goes and grabs
the cat fact and sends it out.

00:14:41.119 --> 00:14:42.910
What's really cool
about this, too, though,

00:14:42.910 --> 00:14:44.860
is you'll notice that we have
multiple services calling

00:14:44.860 --> 00:14:46.004
to the Cat Facts service.

00:14:46.004 --> 00:14:47.920
So we have the chat bot,
which is also calling

00:14:47.920 --> 00:14:49.120
to the Cat Facts service.

00:14:49.120 --> 00:14:51.580
And we could build a whole
fleet of services regarding

00:14:51.580 --> 00:14:54.157
Cat Facts, depending
on the kind of service

00:14:54.157 --> 00:14:55.240
you want to use to use it.

00:14:55.240 --> 00:14:59.260
We can make e-mailers,
SMS, chat bots, IRC bots.

00:14:59.260 --> 00:15:01.330
And they can all use
the same microservice

00:15:01.330 --> 00:15:03.970
that can scale up, based
on how much load there is.

00:15:03.970 --> 00:15:06.250
Maybe we find out that people
don't like email anymore

00:15:06.250 --> 00:15:08.080
and we need to pivot to SMS.

00:15:08.080 --> 00:15:09.050
We're ready to do that.

00:15:09.050 --> 00:15:10.716
And we don't have to
do much refactoring

00:15:10.716 --> 00:15:14.830
to our actual architecture
in order to support that.

00:15:14.830 --> 00:15:16.470
AJA HAMMERLY: So at
this point, let's

00:15:16.470 --> 00:15:19.380
just pretend that Cat
Facts is the new hotness

00:15:19.380 --> 00:15:20.880
and it is wildly
successful and we

00:15:20.880 --> 00:15:23.400
have hundreds of millions of
users getting their cat facts

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:25.660
and cat pictures
on a daily basis.

00:15:25.660 --> 00:15:28.200
So if that happens, which
we hope will happen,

00:15:28.200 --> 00:15:29.160
we're going to be
able to hire someone

00:15:29.160 --> 00:15:30.780
to help us with operations.

00:15:30.780 --> 00:15:33.196
And when I've talked to my
friends who work in operations,

00:15:33.196 --> 00:15:35.800
one of them said that we need
to consider Google Kubernetes

00:15:35.800 --> 00:15:36.300
engine.

00:15:36.300 --> 00:15:37.860
I was talking to
her about it and she

00:15:37.860 --> 00:15:39.690
said that it would give
us more flexibility

00:15:39.690 --> 00:15:41.700
with our architecture,
while retaining

00:15:41.700 --> 00:15:44.474
many of the benefits of App
Engine and Cloud Functions.

00:15:44.474 --> 00:15:46.140
And it would specially
work well for us,

00:15:46.140 --> 00:15:48.450
because we've already moved
toward a microservices

00:15:48.450 --> 00:15:50.970
architecture, with a bunch of
different scalable components.

00:15:53.974 --> 00:15:55.390
MYLES BORINS: So
you may be asking

00:15:55.390 --> 00:15:58.531
yourself, what's a Kubernetes?

00:15:58.531 --> 00:16:01.030
How many people in the audience
have used Kubernetes so far?

00:16:01.030 --> 00:16:01.870
AJA HAMMERLY: OK.

00:16:01.870 --> 00:16:04.161
MYLES BORINS: So you're not
asking what's a Kubernetes?

00:16:04.161 --> 00:16:06.820
But for those who haven't,
Kubernetes is the Greek word,

00:16:06.820 --> 00:16:07.820
I believe, for helmsmen.

00:16:07.820 --> 00:16:09.778
AJA HAMMERLY: Governor,
helmsmen, one of those.

00:16:09.778 --> 00:16:10.750
I don't remember which.

00:16:10.750 --> 00:16:14.080
MYLES BORINS: And it is a
service that's open sourced.

00:16:14.080 --> 00:16:17.230
It's in the CNCF, the Cloud
Native Compute Foundation.

00:16:17.230 --> 00:16:21.550
And it's for orchestrating
and scaling Docker containers

00:16:21.550 --> 00:16:22.520
in production.

00:16:22.520 --> 00:16:24.850
It's a way of setting
up Cloud architecture.

00:16:24.850 --> 00:16:27.850
And it's designed and
based on the Google

00:16:27.850 --> 00:16:29.554
internal architecture work.

00:16:29.554 --> 00:16:30.970
So a lot of the
lessons and things

00:16:30.970 --> 00:16:33.790
that we've learned about
making scalable services

00:16:33.790 --> 00:16:39.820
were all used to inspire the
architecture of Kubernetes.

00:16:39.820 --> 00:16:44.177
On our next slide, we're going
to show what a YAML file looks

00:16:44.177 --> 00:16:45.010
like for Kubernetes.

00:16:45.010 --> 00:16:46.000
And Aja's going tell
you all about that.

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:48.460
AJA HAMMERLY: So Here's an
example Kubernetes deployment

00:16:48.460 --> 00:16:51.044
file for and Nginx container.

00:16:51.044 --> 00:16:52.960
The big thing to be aware
of with Kubernetes--

00:16:52.960 --> 00:16:55.376
and this is the thing that a
lot of folks find challenging

00:16:55.376 --> 00:16:55.960
at first--

00:16:55.960 --> 00:16:58.080
is that unlike when
you're signing up VMs

00:16:58.080 --> 00:17:01.300
and you're doing scripting to
make your VMs reproducible,

00:17:01.300 --> 00:17:02.775
all of that's
focused on the how.

00:17:02.775 --> 00:17:04.900
You want to install this
thing and then this thing.

00:17:04.900 --> 00:17:06.700
And then, you want to
change these ports.

00:17:06.700 --> 00:17:09.250
And you want to set up
this kind of load balancer.

00:17:09.250 --> 00:17:12.790
Kubernetes, you need to describe
what a happy, wonderful, good

00:17:12.790 --> 00:17:15.028
state is for your product.

00:17:15.028 --> 00:17:16.569
This many versions
of this container.

00:17:16.569 --> 00:17:18.110
This many versions
of this container.

00:17:18.110 --> 00:17:19.266
This kind of load balancer.

00:17:19.266 --> 00:17:21.099
And then, what Kubernetes
does for you is it

00:17:21.099 --> 00:17:24.020
does everything in its power
to keep that happy state.

00:17:24.020 --> 00:17:26.640
So if one of your nodes
magically, one of your VM

00:17:26.640 --> 00:17:28.840
gets hit by lightning in
the center of your center--

00:17:28.840 --> 00:17:30.000
it could happen--

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:32.920
Kubernetes will take what
was running on that VM,

00:17:32.920 --> 00:17:35.560
on that node, and it will
put it to other places,

00:17:35.560 --> 00:17:37.780
in a way to make
sure that your app is

00:17:37.780 --> 00:17:40.720
as close to that running
state, that happy state

00:17:40.720 --> 00:17:41.752
that you want it to be.

00:17:41.752 --> 00:17:43.960
And it takes a little bit
of work for a lot of people

00:17:43.960 --> 00:17:45.335
to get their head
around the fact

00:17:45.335 --> 00:17:47.464
that you don't explain
how to get there.

00:17:47.464 --> 00:17:49.630
You use the concepts that
are built into Kubernetes,

00:17:49.630 --> 00:17:53.440
pods, services, deployments,
to describe what a successfully

00:17:53.440 --> 00:17:54.580
running app looks like.

00:17:58.000 --> 00:18:00.300
So Myles mentioned it a bit.

00:18:00.300 --> 00:18:02.700
In that example, I was
actually using the Nginx Docker

00:18:02.700 --> 00:18:04.605
container.

00:18:04.605 --> 00:18:06.360
What's a Docker, was
one of the things

00:18:06.360 --> 00:18:07.860
that I asked when
people were trying

00:18:07.860 --> 00:18:09.180
to teach me about Kubernetes.

00:18:09.180 --> 00:18:13.620
And it is a container format.

00:18:13.620 --> 00:18:18.570
And a container is a
way of packaging up

00:18:18.570 --> 00:18:20.945
runtime and the files and the
dependencies to go with it.

00:18:20.945 --> 00:18:23.403
And the nice thing about it is
you can put multiple of them

00:18:23.403 --> 00:18:23.950
on a VM.

00:18:23.950 --> 00:18:27.250
And you don't have to deal
with dependency conflicts.

00:18:27.250 --> 00:18:29.584
Like I know when I was working
in ops, one of the things

00:18:29.584 --> 00:18:32.166
we had was we had, like, three
different parts of the service,

00:18:32.166 --> 00:18:33.190
different microservices.

00:18:33.190 --> 00:18:35.356
And they each were using
slightly different versions

00:18:35.356 --> 00:18:38.120
of various Ruby gems or slightly
different versions of various C

00:18:38.120 --> 00:18:39.384
libraries.

00:18:39.384 --> 00:18:41.800
And installing them together,
side by side, was a problem.

00:18:41.800 --> 00:18:43.240
And then, we had to get like
three different dev teams

00:18:43.240 --> 00:18:44.860
to actually agree
on which version

00:18:44.860 --> 00:18:46.337
we were going to standardize on.

00:18:46.337 --> 00:18:47.920
With Docker, you
could just package up

00:18:47.920 --> 00:18:50.350
an app with its dependencies
and run it next to an app

00:18:50.350 --> 00:18:52.141
with slightly different,
or very different,

00:18:52.141 --> 00:18:54.290
dependencies on
the same machine.

00:18:54.290 --> 00:18:56.080
And you get much
better utilization.

00:18:56.080 --> 00:18:56.960
And you get safety.

00:18:56.960 --> 00:18:59.950
The other nice thing about
using containers and Docker is

00:18:59.950 --> 00:19:02.200
that, since you're
packaging a container,

00:19:02.200 --> 00:19:04.690
you seem to have less
problems with the works in dev

00:19:04.690 --> 00:19:06.400
doesn't work in prod
situation, which

00:19:06.400 --> 00:19:09.241
I know I've spent many hours
banging my head against.

00:19:09.241 --> 00:19:10.740
And when you read
about this, you're

00:19:10.740 --> 00:19:13.711
going to see stuff about
namespaces and cgroups and all

00:19:13.711 --> 00:19:14.210
that.

00:19:14.210 --> 00:19:19.840
But the big thing is
running application

00:19:19.840 --> 00:19:21.280
with its dependencies
and its file

00:19:21.280 --> 00:19:24.167
system all packaged up neatly.

00:19:24.167 --> 00:19:26.250
MYLES BORINS: So what you
can see up on the screen

00:19:26.250 --> 00:19:30.021
now is a fairly
straightforward Docker file.

00:19:30.021 --> 00:19:32.270
We're starting from an image
that's actually published

00:19:32.270 --> 00:19:34.040
by the Node.js project.

00:19:34.040 --> 00:19:38.930
Carbon is the 8.x
branch for LTS.

00:19:38.930 --> 00:19:41.482
And if you go from
Node.js carbon-alpine.

00:19:41.482 --> 00:19:43.940
What that means is we're going
to grab the latest published

00:19:43.940 --> 00:19:47.940
version of 8.x of Node running
on an Alpine-based operating

00:19:47.940 --> 00:19:48.440
system.

00:19:48.440 --> 00:19:51.380
And Alpine is a very,
very thin operating system

00:19:51.380 --> 00:19:53.780
that's been designed
specifically for running

00:19:53.780 --> 00:19:55.680
in Docker containers.

00:19:55.680 --> 00:19:58.190
If you're looking for fast
boots and thin images,

00:19:58.190 --> 00:20:00.140
Alpine is a good
place to get started.

00:20:00.140 --> 00:20:02.450
We're copying the file
system from the folder

00:20:02.450 --> 00:20:05.660
that we're running in
directly into the image.

00:20:05.660 --> 00:20:09.050
We're going to run the command,
NPM install production.

00:20:09.050 --> 00:20:11.600
That production flag makes sure
that you're dev dependencies

00:20:11.600 --> 00:20:13.010
don't get installed.

00:20:13.010 --> 00:20:15.019
And it's important to
run all of the commands

00:20:15.019 --> 00:20:17.060
to set up your environment
before you're actually

00:20:17.060 --> 00:20:17.977
running in production.

00:20:17.977 --> 00:20:20.435
So this is going to make sure
that all the dependencies you

00:20:20.435 --> 00:20:22.760
need are there, that there's
nothing in that image

00:20:22.760 --> 00:20:25.530
that you don't need for
running in production.

00:20:25.530 --> 00:20:28.422
Then, the command NPM start is
going to boot everything up.

00:20:28.422 --> 00:20:30.505
So that command gets run
when the Docker container

00:20:30.505 --> 00:20:32.120
is started.

00:20:32.120 --> 00:20:35.210
NPM start is a really great
way to boot things up.

00:20:35.210 --> 00:20:37.520
It's something you put in
your package JSON script.

00:20:37.520 --> 00:20:40.220
If anyone has more questions
about kind of best practices

00:20:40.220 --> 00:20:41.930
for setting up
node applications,

00:20:41.930 --> 00:20:45.294
please feel free to
grab me after the talk.

00:20:45.294 --> 00:20:46.710
AJA HAMMERLY: So
one of the things

00:20:46.710 --> 00:20:48.969
I mentioned in my definition
of Docker is cgroups.

00:20:48.969 --> 00:20:50.760
And you might be
thinking, what's a cgroup?

00:20:50.760 --> 00:20:54.240
And that's out of
scope for this talk.

00:20:54.240 --> 00:20:55.770
So you can come talk.

00:20:55.770 --> 00:20:57.570
There's containers,
office hours,

00:20:57.570 --> 00:21:01.170
and there's also office hours
on Google Clouds Open Source

00:21:01.170 --> 00:21:03.150
projects in the
office hours space.

00:21:03.150 --> 00:21:05.157
And folks from the
Kubernetes project

00:21:05.157 --> 00:21:06.990
will be there, if you
want to talk in detail

00:21:06.990 --> 00:21:09.830
about the implementation.

00:21:09.830 --> 00:21:12.170
So why would you make
the switch to Kubernetes?

00:21:12.170 --> 00:21:14.720
This is the question I get the
most when I'm out in the field

00:21:14.720 --> 00:21:17.169
talking to folks at events
or I'm at dev ops meetups.

00:21:17.169 --> 00:21:19.460
And the first thing I want
to say is you don't have to.

00:21:19.460 --> 00:21:21.230
There's a lot of folks
out there who are like,

00:21:21.230 --> 00:21:22.550
Kubernetes is the new hotness.

00:21:22.550 --> 00:21:24.440
I have to use Kubernetes.

00:21:24.440 --> 00:21:28.820
And if what you have
works for you, great.

00:21:28.820 --> 00:21:31.580
App Engine, Cloud Functions,
or VMs or any combination

00:21:31.580 --> 00:21:33.680
of those, like we had
with our Cat Facts app,

00:21:33.680 --> 00:21:36.590
they're a fine way to run
your app indefinitely.

00:21:36.590 --> 00:21:38.580
If it works, don't fix it.

00:21:38.580 --> 00:21:40.442
But containers provide
some advantages

00:21:40.442 --> 00:21:41.900
around portability,
predictability,

00:21:41.900 --> 00:21:43.460
and utilization.

00:21:43.460 --> 00:21:45.995
And I've seen a lot
of success with teams

00:21:45.995 --> 00:21:47.120
that have switched to this.

00:21:47.120 --> 00:21:48.980
The big thing that I've
seen from my dev ops friends

00:21:48.980 --> 00:21:51.620
is that they're getting better
utilization out of their VMs.

00:21:51.620 --> 00:21:54.260
They're able to use all of
the CPU and all of the memory

00:21:54.260 --> 00:21:55.460
that they provision.

00:21:55.460 --> 00:21:58.460
It's less of an issue with GCP,
because Google Compute Engine

00:21:58.460 --> 00:22:02.810
lets you specify and make custom
VMs that exactly fit your app.

00:22:02.810 --> 00:22:06.080
But if you have things that
are really variable in load

00:22:06.080 --> 00:22:08.180
or you've got a
bunch of VMs that all

00:22:08.180 --> 00:22:10.310
look the same, for
whatever reason,

00:22:10.310 --> 00:22:12.949
containers might be a way
to solve that problem.

00:22:12.949 --> 00:22:14.740
And if you've decided
to go with containers

00:22:14.740 --> 00:22:16.336
for any of these
or other reasons,

00:22:16.336 --> 00:22:18.460
Kubernetes is a great way
to manage the bin packing

00:22:18.460 --> 00:22:19.930
problem of putting things
there and making sure

00:22:19.930 --> 00:22:21.250
that everything stays up.

00:22:21.250 --> 00:22:24.760
I really like that if you
specify known good state,

00:22:24.760 --> 00:22:27.430
it will take care of making
that known good state maintained

00:22:27.430 --> 00:22:29.119
for you, even if
something weird happens

00:22:29.119 --> 00:22:30.160
to some of your machines.

00:22:33.430 --> 00:22:35.280
MYLES BORINS: So some
concluding thoughts

00:22:35.280 --> 00:22:38.430
about all of the things
that we just said.

00:22:38.430 --> 00:22:42.270
Google Cloud Platform is kind
of like a delicious dim sum

00:22:42.270 --> 00:22:43.780
buffet.

00:22:43.780 --> 00:22:46.490
And bear with me on this.

00:22:46.490 --> 00:22:48.260
You have lots of
different options.

00:22:48.260 --> 00:22:51.410
And you can pick them all
depending on your tastes.

00:22:51.410 --> 00:22:53.110
As you need more, they're there.

00:22:53.110 --> 00:22:54.050
They're on the cart.

00:22:54.050 --> 00:22:54.740
They're steamed.

00:22:54.740 --> 00:22:55.834
They're ready to go.

00:22:55.834 --> 00:22:56.750
You need more shu mai.

00:22:56.750 --> 00:22:58.160
We've got more shu mai.

00:22:58.160 --> 00:22:59.964
And it's really easy to share.

00:22:59.964 --> 00:23:01.880
You have big projects,
you can add more people

00:23:01.880 --> 00:23:02.570
to the projects.

00:23:02.570 --> 00:23:04.860
They can grab the
dumplings they want.

00:23:04.860 --> 00:23:08.269
Aja, now, I'm really hungry.

00:23:08.269 --> 00:23:09.560
AJA HAMMERLY: Almost lunchtime.

00:23:09.560 --> 00:23:11.420
MYLES BORINS: Yeah, as you can
tell, I think almost as much

00:23:11.420 --> 00:23:13.550
about dumplings as I
think about compute.

00:23:13.550 --> 00:23:15.159
But I also like to
not have to think

00:23:15.159 --> 00:23:16.450
about how I make the dumplings.

00:23:16.450 --> 00:23:17.510
I just want to eat them.

00:23:17.510 --> 00:23:19.551
And that really becomes
one of the great benefits

00:23:19.551 --> 00:23:21.150
about using a
platform like this.

00:23:21.150 --> 00:23:22.712
If you need to
focus on your MVP,

00:23:22.712 --> 00:23:24.170
if you need to
focus on the product

00:23:24.170 --> 00:23:25.820
that you're getting
out of the door

00:23:25.820 --> 00:23:27.775
and your product
isn't operations,

00:23:27.775 --> 00:23:29.900
you may want to be just
using all these things that

00:23:29.900 --> 00:23:30.920
are ready to go.

00:23:30.920 --> 00:23:33.510
You can iterate off
of them as necessary.

00:23:33.510 --> 00:23:35.270
And you can make
architecture decisions

00:23:35.270 --> 00:23:37.820
to make sure that you are
not super locked into things

00:23:37.820 --> 00:23:39.166
that are harder to move.

00:23:39.166 --> 00:23:41.540
But we have a lot of different
products available for you

00:23:41.540 --> 00:23:44.570
today to run your compute in
all sorts of different ways

00:23:44.570 --> 00:23:46.776
based on your needs.

00:23:46.776 --> 00:23:49.400
AJA HAMMERLY: So we went through
a lot of stuff really quickly.

00:23:49.400 --> 00:23:51.040
And one of the things I want
to make sure everyone knows

00:23:51.040 --> 00:23:52.834
is that if you choose
one of these things,

00:23:52.834 --> 00:23:55.000
it doesn't mean you can't
change your mind later on.

00:23:55.000 --> 00:23:56.770
All of these things
play well together.

00:23:56.770 --> 00:23:58.859
Projects that use a
lot of them are here.

00:23:58.859 --> 00:24:00.400
Here's kind of the
high-level summary

00:24:00.400 --> 00:24:01.608
of what we just talked about.

00:24:01.608 --> 00:24:04.780
So we've got Google Compute
Engine, virtual machines

00:24:04.780 --> 00:24:05.590
in custom sizes.

00:24:05.590 --> 00:24:07.715
I was talking to someone
and they had done the math

00:24:07.715 --> 00:24:10.900
and there's over 6,000
ways, I think, to configure,

00:24:10.900 --> 00:24:13.584
6,000 different flavors of VM.

00:24:13.584 --> 00:24:15.750
This is good when you are
already familiar with VMs.

00:24:15.750 --> 00:24:17.425
Who's shipped something on a VM?

00:24:17.425 --> 00:24:19.150
I know I have lots
and lots of times.

00:24:19.150 --> 00:24:19.649
Wow.

00:24:19.649 --> 00:24:22.584
That's actually fewer
folks than I expected.

00:24:22.584 --> 00:24:24.250
Or if you just want
to pick up something

00:24:24.250 --> 00:24:26.291
that you're running in a
VM on your local machine

00:24:26.291 --> 00:24:28.210
and shove it into production.

00:24:28.210 --> 00:24:29.860
We have Google App Engine.

00:24:29.860 --> 00:24:31.810
This is a serverless
application platform.

00:24:31.810 --> 00:24:35.020
It's good when you want a no
ops solution to running your app

00:24:35.020 --> 00:24:36.520
and you want fully
managed services.

00:24:36.520 --> 00:24:37.978
You want networking
taking care of.

00:24:37.978 --> 00:24:39.500
You want autoscaling.

00:24:39.500 --> 00:24:44.720
You want the ability to use
images that are known good

00:24:44.720 --> 00:24:46.161
that Google has
endorsed and we're

00:24:46.161 --> 00:24:47.410
making sure we're maintaining.

00:24:47.410 --> 00:24:49.850
We're making sure we're
patching and things like that.

00:24:49.850 --> 00:24:52.460
We've got Google Cloud Functions
for event-driven serverless

00:24:52.460 --> 00:24:56.080
applications or for event-driven
parts of your application.

00:24:56.080 --> 00:24:58.580
This is good for tasks that
need high scale some of the time

00:24:58.580 --> 00:24:59.600
and are largely stateless.

00:24:59.600 --> 00:25:01.350
It's especially good
if you have something

00:25:01.350 --> 00:25:04.010
that you need to run a lot at
once and not a lot later on,

00:25:04.010 --> 00:25:06.260
because you can shove as
much through that pipe as you

00:25:06.260 --> 00:25:07.339
need to, or as little.

00:25:07.339 --> 00:25:08.630
It depends on what your use is.

00:25:08.630 --> 00:25:10.530
And it's really great for that.

00:25:10.530 --> 00:25:12.800
And then, we have Google
Kubernetes engine.

00:25:12.800 --> 00:25:15.920
And this is managed Kubernetes
on Google Compute Engine.

00:25:15.920 --> 00:25:18.020
This is absolutely one
of the easiest ways

00:25:18.020 --> 00:25:20.097
to get started with
containers and Kubernetes.

00:25:20.097 --> 00:25:21.180
This is how I got started.

00:25:21.180 --> 00:25:23.263
And it's really, really
good, because we take care

00:25:23.263 --> 00:25:24.590
of a lot of the stuff for you.

00:25:24.590 --> 00:25:26.450
While there's lots of folks
involved in the Kubernetes

00:25:26.450 --> 00:25:28.220
project, both inside
and outside Google,

00:25:28.220 --> 00:25:30.020
Google is still very involved.

00:25:30.020 --> 00:25:32.937
And the team that builds
Google Kubernetes engine

00:25:32.937 --> 00:25:35.270
works with the team that's
working on Kubernetes to make

00:25:35.270 --> 00:25:37.244
sure that everything is smooth.

00:25:37.244 --> 00:25:38.410
So we want to say thank you.

00:25:38.410 --> 00:25:40.650
And before we actually say thank
you to you all for attending,

00:25:40.650 --> 00:25:42.090
I would actually like to
really thank all the folks who

00:25:42.090 --> 00:25:43.360
are making this possible.

00:25:43.360 --> 00:25:45.212
We've got fantastic
staff running the lights

00:25:45.212 --> 00:25:46.920
and running the camera
for the Livestream

00:25:46.920 --> 00:25:48.000
and producing in this room.

00:25:48.000 --> 00:25:49.260
So if you all could give
them a round of applause,

00:25:49.260 --> 00:25:49.926
I'd be grateful.

00:25:49.926 --> 00:25:51.210
[APPLAUSE]

00:25:55.540 --> 00:25:58.000
And thank you all for
coming and giving us

00:25:58.000 --> 00:26:01.150
45 minutes of your wonderful
day today at Google I/O.

00:26:01.150 --> 00:26:03.380
I hope you have a
wonderful afternoon.

00:26:03.380 --> 00:26:04.426
MYLES BORINS: Thank you.

00:26:04.426 --> 00:26:08.800
